\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}
\title{Community Solar Sharing:\\Year-One Audit}
\author{D.~Whitfield and A.~Serrano}
\date{February 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
The cooperative's shared array completed its first full year of
operation in December, and this audit collects what we learned.
The site, summarized in Section 2, combines rooftop and carport
panels behind a single meter.
Later sections report quarterly findings, the cost allocation they
support, and recommendations for year two.
\section{Site Background}
\label{sec:background}
The array pairs 84 rooftop panels with 60 carport panels on the
community centre, for a nameplate capacity of 61 kilowatts.
Members buy shares in units of 250 watts, and the metering approach
of Section 3 credits each share against the member's retail bill.
Orientation is near-south for the rooftop blocks and southwest for
the carport, which smooths the afternoon ramp.
\section{Metering Method}
\label{sec:metering}
The utility meter records net export at fifteen-minute resolution.
We allocate that export to members in proportion to their shares,
and the quarterly findings in Section 4 show how the allocation
behaves across seasons.
\subsection{Hardware}
\label{sec:hardware}
A single revenue-grade meter sits at the point of common coupling,
with a data logger that pushes readings to the cooperative's server
each night.
\subsection{Data Cleaning}
\label{sec:cleaning}
Gaps shorter than two hours are filled by linear interpolation;
longer gaps, mostly from two storm outages, are flagged and excluded
from the allocation denominator.
\subsection{Allocation Rule}
\label{sec:rule}
Each member's credit is the member's share fraction times the
metered export for the billing period, valued at the retail tariff.
\section{Quarterly Findings}
\label{sec:findings}
Generation tracked the irradiance forecast within six percent in
every quarter except the storm-heavy third quarter.
Section 5 translates these findings into the cost allocation that
members see on their bills.
Soiling losses were largest after the dry spring and were recovered
by the first heavy rain of June.
\section{Cost Allocation}
\label{sec:allocation}
Members received credits covering 71 percent of the array's deemed
output on average, with the remainder reflecting vacancy in two
share tranches for part of the year.
Section 6 turns the allocation results into recommendations for the
board.
Administrative overhead stayed under four percent of revenue.
\section{Recommendations}
\label{sec:recommendations}
We recommend filling the vacant tranches before summer, adding a
second meter at the carport inverter to isolate its losses, and
publishing the allocation workbook alongside this audit.
A mid-year review should revisit the cleaning schedule if the dry
spring pattern repeats.
\end{document}

